    "Seasons": {
      "//": "A run of the world, from everybody starting level to everybody starting level again. ON, and with a date: Season 1 is a raid race running 2026-09-01 to 2026-11-30 UTC, and the winner is whoever takes the most cash, weed, and coke from other players before the clock runs out. Enabled is what makes the clock actually roll the world when it runs out, and rolling deletes every empire in it - so it is flipped with a hand on the switch, and StartsAtUtc is set alongside it precisely so the end date is a date somebody chose rather than whenever the first request happened to land. Beware the two together: enabling this while EndsAtUtc is already in the past rolls the world on the very next request. Both are readable from the admin config panel, though only Enabled and LengthDays can be changed there without a restart.",
      "Enabled": true,
      "//StartsAtUtc": "When season one begins, as an absolute UTC instant - for example \"2026-09-15T18:00:00Z\". Null means it begins whenever the first request asking about it lands, so ninety days can end up counted from a deploy-time health check and the end date is whatever the database happens to say. Set it and the window becomes announceable: the start is fixed, the end is the start plus LengthDays, and a restart changes neither because neither was ever held in the process. Only season one reads it; every season after starts when the one before it ended.",
      "StartsAtUtc": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z",
      "LengthDays": 90,
      "ChampionHeadStart": 50000,
      "TopThreeHeadStart": 25000,
      "TopTenHeadStart": 10000,
      "NameFormat": "Season {0}"
    },
    "Arrests": {
      "//": "Getting swept up working the streets. The chance is priced on the crew actually on the street, so it scales with the operation rather than being a flat trickle, and nobody under FreeCrewOnStreet is ever taken - the same floor the heat rules keep, for the same reason. Bail is deliberately dearer than hiring the same head again: for anonymous crew you pay it to keep the morale rather than to save money, and for a named pimp you pay it because another pimp is not that pimp.",
      "FreeCrewOnStreet": 20,
      "ChancePerCrewPerShift": 0.004,
      "MaxChancePerShift": 0.5,
      "HeatScaleDivisor": 100,
      "MinTakenPercent": 0.01,
      "MaxTakenPercent": 0.03,
      "PimpTakenChance": 0.12,
      "BailWindowHours": 6,
      "BailPerHoe": 3000,
      "BailPerThug": 6000,
      "BailPerPimp": 25000,
      "AbandonMoralePerHead": 0.8,
      "MaxAbandonMoralePenalty": 20,
      "AbandonLoyaltyPenalty": 6,
      "TalkLoyaltyThreshold": 50,
      "TalkChance": 0.4,
      "TalkHeat": 12
    },
    "Bank": {
      "//": "A trip to the bank costs turns, and the charge is on the visit rather than on the amount or the direction. Moves inside the grace window after a paid trip are free, so one visit is one charge however many times money changes hands during it. The window is fixed rather than sliding: it opens when the trip is paid for and is not pushed along by the free moves inside it.",
      "TripTurnCost": 2,
      "TripGraceMinutes": 5
    },
    "Casino": {
      "//": "The Casino District as a playable floor. Bets come from cash on hand and winnings return to cash on hand, so chips do not become a second protected bank.",
      "Enabled": true,
      "HistoryDepth": 8,
      "RepPerDollarWagered": 0.005,
      "SpinTurnCost": 1,
      "//Comps": "Standing gates the menu and comps pay for it. Comps are rated on the wager rather than on the loss, the way a real players' club rates play - a hundredth of the stake, which returns roughly a fifth of what the house expects to hold. It is what a pull costs a turn and gives nothing back in return: slots are negative expectation at every stake by design, so the reason to play cannot be the money.",
      "CompsPerDollarWagered": 0.01,
      "CompRewards": [
        { "Key": "room", "Name": "A room upstairs", "Blurb": "The house keeps one for people who play. Sleep it off and start again.", "Cost": 500, "Turns": 25, "MinCasinoRepLevel": 1 },
        { "Key": "cage", "Name": "The cage settles up", "Blurb": "Walk to the window and take what you are owed in cash.", "Cost": 1000, "Cash": 1000, "MinCasinoRepLevel": 2 },
        { "Key": "word", "Name": "A word with the law", "Blurb": "Somebody the house knows makes a call, and a file gets thinner.", "Cost": 2500, "Heat": 20, "MinCasinoRepLevel": 3 },
        { "Key": "suite", "Name": "The suite, and a car home", "Blurb": "The floor manager stops calling you sir and starts using your name.", "Cost": 10000, "Turns": 100, "Cash": 5000, "Heat": 40, "MinCasinoRepLevel": 4 }
      ],
      "Jackpot": {
        "//": "A slice off every wager on a machine, pooled until four Vaults land anywhere on the fifteen cells with every lane bought - about one spin in eighty thousand. Three would have been one in twenty-four hundred on a grid this size, which is a pot that drops before it has grown. The slice comes out of what the paytable would have returned, so the house holds the same share either way; it is only paid back in one lump instead of a hundred small ones.",
        "Enabled": true,
        "ContributionPercent": 1.0,
        "Symbol": "vault",
        "SymbolsRequired": 4,
        "RequireAllPaylines": true
      },
      "Levels": [
        { "Level": 1, "Name": "Walk-In", "Rep": 0 },
        { "Level": 2, "Name": "Regular", "Rep": 100 },
        { "Level": 3, "Name": "High Roller", "Rep": 1000 },
        { "Level": 4, "Name": "House Name", "Rep": 5000 }
      ],
      "//SlotMachines": "Five columns by three rows, nine lanes, and a lane pays on the run it opens with from the left. Each machine turns its own reel. Two things rise together as you climb, both deliberately: volatility, from a grinder to a room where almost nothing happens until it does, and the return itself, because a real floor holds most on its cheapest machines and least in the high-limit room. There is no win cap - the paytable is the ceiling.",
      "SlotMachines": [
        {
